The map shows the difference in value (refined minus baseline) for 2018. Red cells gain value with the refinement and blue cells lose it.

diff_data &lt;- (refined_10km[[4]] - base_10km[[4]]) |&gt;
  setNames("difference") |&gt;
  as.data.frame(xy = TRUE) |&gt;
  filter(abs(difference) &gt; 0)

diff_limit &lt;- as.numeric(quantile(abs(diff_data$difference), 0.99))

ggplot(diff_data) +
  geom_tile(aes(x = x, y = y, fill = pmax(pmin(difference, diff_limit), -diff_limit))) +
  geom_sf(data = countries, fill = NA, linewidth = 0.1) +
  scale_fill_gradient2(
    low = "#2166ac", mid = "white", high = "#b2182b",
    name = "Million euro\n(2010 prices)\nper 10 km cell"
  ) +
  coord_sf(xlim = c(2.6e6, 6.6e6), ylim = c(1.4e6, 5.4e6), datum = NA) +
  theme_void()








The refined maps depend on a second dataset that was mapped independently of FAWS, so they are offered as an option and the baseline remains the main result.

export_table(
  comparison |&gt; rename_with(\(x) gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9]+", "_", x)),
  "corine_refinement_value_comparison_with_baseline",
  dir = refine_results_dir
)

This page documents the R routine that updates the wood provision ecosystem service for the European Union and makes it spatially explicit. Wood provision is the ecosystem contribution to the growth of trees, and it is measured here with the net annual increment (NAI) of the forest available for wood supply (FAWS), in thousand cubic metres over bark (Eurostat, 2026a; La Notte et al., 2021). Each step is written so that the team can read it, rerun it from the repository alone and reuse the parts that generalise.
The routine follows the client instructions and has four components, in this order:

the statistical component, which builds a complete country-by-year NAI series from Eurostat for_vol_efa (Eurostat, 2026b) for 2000 to 2024, and reports the years 2000, 2006, 2012, 2018 and 2024;
the overuse check, which compares removals with NAI and flags country-years in which removals exceed NAI;
the spatial component, which spreads each national NAI over the map of forest available for wood supply in 2020 (European Commission, Joint Research Centre, 2026), so that the pixels of a country add up to its national value;
an optional refinement, which repeats the spatial step with Corine Land Cover forest of each epoch and compares it with the baseline, with outputs kept apart;
the exports, which write the tables and maps to data/outputs/ for download.







ImportantThe Excel file in data/inputs/eurostat/ is not the input



The workbook for_vol_efa$defaultview_spreadsheet.xlsx is the default view of the dataset. It reports million euro for the indicator “Forest” in 2015 to 2024 and holds no volumes, so it cannot supply NAI or removals in thousand cubic metres. The routine downloads the right slice of for_vol_efa with the eurostat package (script scripts/03_download_eurostat.R) and keeps the workbook only as provenance.








NoteTwo reports are consulted for the method



INCA Part III (La Notte et al., 2021) defines the service and the NAI proxy, and INCA Part II (Vallecillo et al., 2019) (chapter 4) describes how national flows are spatialised. The second reference in the client instructions (KS-01-26-019-EN-N) is the Eurostat ecosystem accounts handbook (Eurostat, 2026a), whose wood chapter treats FAWS as a cultivated context measured by net increment and forest not available for wood supply (FNAWS) as an uncultivated context measured by removals. Neither report gives a formula for timber overuse, so the check below applies the definition in the client instructions (removals greater than NAI). Source notes are in research/.








NotePaths are relative to the project root



_quarto.yml sets execute-dir: project, so every path below resolves from the repository root regardless of where the .qmd file sits. There is no need for here::here() or setwd().

Loading Eurostat data
The extract has one row per country, year, flow (NAI or RMOV) and forest class. FAWS is forest available for wood supply, FNAWS forest not available, and FOR total forest. Only the 27 member states are kept, and the EU aggregate published by Eurostat is held aside to check the totals later.

for_vol_efa &lt;- readRDS(eurostat_file)

countries &lt;- st_read(countries_file, quiet = TRUE)
eu27 &lt;- countries$geo[countries$eu27]

eu_aggregate &lt;- for_vol_efa |&gt;
  filter(geo == "EU27_2020")

for_vol_efa &lt;- for_vol_efa |&gt;
  filter(geo %in% eu27)

A small function picks one series of the extract. It keeps the later steps to a single line.

pick_series &lt;- function(flow_name, class_name) {
  for_vol_efa |&gt;
    filter(flow == flow_name, forest_class == class_name)
}

How much was reported
The table counts the member states that reported each series in the five reporting years. NAI of FAWS is reported by very few countries before 2018, whereas NAI of total forest and removals cover nearly all of them. This is the reason for the gap-filling rule in the next section.

for_vol_efa |&gt;
  filter(
    year %in% years_report,
    forest_class %in% c("FAWS", "FOR")
  ) |&gt;
  count(flow, forest_class, year) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = year, values_from = n, values_fill = 0) |&gt;
  rename(Flow = flow, `Forest class` = forest_class) |&gt;
  show_table(digits = 0)

FlowForest class20062012201820242000NAIFAWS261420NAIFOR262626226RMOVFAWS262626226RMOVFOR262626226

Gap filling NAI of FAWS
The INCA plugin fills the NAI of FAWS in the order below, and the routine does the same so that results are comparable with the plugin. The helper fill_series() applies the rules and labels each value with its origin.

Use the NAI of FAWS where the country reported it (reported).
Otherwise use the NAI of total forest (fallback).
Remaining gaps take the mean of the nearest earlier and later value of the same country (interpolated). Gaps at the start or end of a series repeat the nearest value (carried).


nai_filled &lt;- fill_series(
  primary = pick_series("NAI", "FAWS"),
  fallback = pick_series("NAI", "FOR"),
  geos = eu27,
  years = years_all
)

The table shows how many of the 27 values of each reporting year come from each rule. The 2024 row is dominated by carried values, because Eurostat holds 2024 for very few countries.

nai_filled |&gt;
  filter(year %in% years_report) |&gt;
  count(year, source) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = source, values_from = n, values_fill = 0) |&gt;
  rename(Year = year) |&gt;
  show_table(digits = 0)

Yearfallbackcarriedreported20002610200624122012201620181211420240252

Check against the Eurostat aggregate
The sum of the 27 country values is compared with the EU27 aggregate that Eurostat publishes for the same indicator. The aggregate for FAWS is Eurostat’s own estimate and differs from the sum of the reported country values, so some differences are expected. The table is a plausibility check, and values are in million cubic metres.

eu_check &lt;- nai_filled |&gt;
  group_by(year) |&gt;
  summarise(`Sum of countries` = sum(value, na.rm = TRUE) / 1000) |&gt;
  left_join(
    eu_aggregate |&gt;
      filter(flow == "NAI", forest_class %in% c("FAWS", "FOR")) |&gt;
      mutate(
        series = paste("Eurostat EU27", forest_class),
        million_m3 = thousand_m3 / 1000
      ) |&gt;
      select(year, series, million_m3) |&gt;
      pivot_wider(names_from = series, values_from = million_m3),
    by = "year"
  ) |&gt;
  filter(year %in% years_report) |&gt;
  rename(Year = year)

show_table(eu_check, digits = 0)

